<GuidePage title="Minimize the Wires - App Router Guide" root="..">
<div class="kicker">Chapter 13 · Part C: Decouple the Parts</div>
<h1>Minimize the Wires</h1>
<p class="lede">
  Every prop, callback, and context read is a wire: a dependency connecting two parts of
  the UI. Wires are not free: each one couples a component's future to its neighbors'.
  This chapter is about keeping components independent, and why repetition beats plumbing.
</p>

<h2 id="cost">13.1 The cost of a wire</h2>
<p>
  When a parent fetches data and passes it down, the parent stops being a layout concern
  and becomes a <strong>data router</strong>. It must know what every descendant needs,
  fetch the union of it, and thread it through. And from that moment, three components
  change together forever. Child B needs one more field? You edit Child B, the parent's
  fetch, and the prop signature in between. Multiply by every feature for the life of the
  product and the parent becomes what we'll call a <em>coupling layer</em>: a component
  whose main job is re-wiring everyone else's requirements.
</p>
<p>
  The rule that falls out of this is blunt:
  <strong>never pass a child anything the parent does not itself need.</strong>
  <Chip k="react" />
  <Chip k="critical" /> If the parent isn't using the data
  (isn't branching on it, isn't laying it out), the parent is just a wire, and wires
  should be deleted. Legitimate exceptions: identity (an <code>id</code> the child will
  use to ask its own questions), composition (<code>children</code> and other slots), and
  configuration the parent genuinely owns (a variant, a size).
</p>

