<Fragment slot="caption">
Why wires cost more than maintenance. The wired component's requirements live in its
    host, so every move re-plumbs them; the self-contained component names its data and
    can be placed on any page, in any layout, and the dedupe layers make its repeated
    question free.
</Fragment>
</Diagram>

<p>
  Portability is what makes a codebase <em>malleable</em>: pages stop being wiring
  diagrams and become compositions. The mechanism that keeps compositions flexible across
  the server/client boundary is the <strong>slot</strong>: any JSX-valued prop
  (<code>children</code> included). Slots work in <em>both directions</em>, and the two
  directions have different rules worth keeping straight. A <strong>server component can
  use client components freely</strong>: import them, render them, pass them into other
  components' slots; that direction is always allowed. A <strong>client component cannot
  import a server component</strong>, but it can <em>receive</em> one through a slot
  (<a href="05-client-server-boundaries.html#children-pattern">§5.4</a>): the server
  renders the content first, and the client shell places the finished output without ever
  owning it.
</p>

<Snippet>
<pre class="code" is:raw><span class="filename">app/programs/page.tsx - a server page slots BOTH kinds into a client shell</span><span class="tok-kw">export default function</span> <span class="tok-fn">Page</span>() {
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ResizableSplit</span>
      <span class="tok-com">{/* client leaf into a client shell's slot */}</span>
      <span class="tok-prop">left</span>={&lt;<span class="tok-fn">ProgramFilters</span> /&gt;}
      <span class="tok-com">{/* SERVER component into the same client shell:
          rendered on the server, passed through as output */}</span>
      <span class="tok-prop">right</span>={&lt;<span class="tok-fn">ProgramTable</span> /&gt;}
    /&gt;
  );
}</pre>
<CodeCaption>
  The page is a server component, so it can compose freely: it imports the client shell
  and the client leaf, renders the server table, and hands all of them to slots.
</CodeCaption>
</Snippet>

<Snippet>
<pre class="code" is:raw><span class="filename">components/layout/ResizableSplit.tsx - client shell with two slots</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;

<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ResizableSplit</span>({ left, right }: <span class="tok-fn">ResizableSplitProps</span>) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> [ratio, setRatio] = <span class="tok-fn">useState</span>(<span class="tok-str">0.3</span>);
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;div <span class="tok-prop">className</span>=<span class="tok-str">"flex h-full"</span>&gt;
      &lt;div <span class="tok-prop">style</span>={{ flexBasis: <span class="tok-str">`${ratio * 100}%`</span> }}&gt;{left}&lt;/div&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">DragHandle</span> <span class="tok-prop">onDrag</span>={setRatio} /&gt;
      &lt;div <span class="tok-prop">className</span>=<span class="tok-str">"min-w-0 grow"</span>&gt;{right}&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  );
}</pre>
<CodeCaption>
  The shell owns exactly one thing: the divider. It has no idea one of its slots is
  server-rendered HTML and the other is an interactive client leaf, which is the point:
  slots make the boundary invisible to the component that hosts them.
</CodeCaption>
</Snippet>

<p>
  Now notice what this composition buys. <code>ProgramTable</code> can move to the
  dashboard, a drawer, or a report page and it brings its data with it.
  <code>ProgramFilters</code> can be dropped next to any table that reads the same search
  params. And the shell can be swapped (split panes today, tabs next quarter) without
  touching either occupant, because neither occupant ever knew about the other. Three
  components, zero wires among them: every one of them is portable independently.
</p>

<h2 id="rules">The rules</h2>
<table class="tbl">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Rule</th>
      <th>Scope</th>
      <th>Importance</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>
        <strong>Never pass a child anything the parent doesn't itself need.</strong>
        Identity, slots, and parent-owned config are the exceptions.
      </td>
      <td><Chip k="react" /></td>
      <td><Chip k="critical" /></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>
        Don't pass external data to children in parallel:
        <strong>let each child ask the deduped data layer itself</strong>.
      </td>
      <td><Chip k="next" /></td>
      <td><Chip k="very" /></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Prefer <strong>repetition backed by dedupe</strong> over shared plumbing.</td>
      <td><Chip k="next" /></td>
      <td><Chip k="very" /></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>
        App-level communication goes <strong>through the URL or the cache</strong>, not
        callbacks up the tree.
      </td>
      <td><Chip k="react" /></td>
      <td><Chip k="critical" /></td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

